[
    {
        "content": "<p>I have the following context in my proof, where <code>z</code> is type <code>ℤ</code> and where <code>a</code> is type <code>ℤ × ℤ</code> (and some hypotheses that I think are unrelated were excluded from this list):<br>\n<code>q : ℂ\nthis : Summable fun z ↦ ‖q ^ z ^ 2‖\n⊢ Summable fun a ↦ ‖q ^ a.1 ^ 2 * q ^ a.2 ^ 2‖</code><br>\nNote that the norm in <code>this</code> is type <code>Complex.instNorm</code>, and the norm in the goal is type <code>NonUnitalSeminormedRing.toSeminormedAddCommGroup.toNorm</code>.</p>\n<p>The solution that I'm going to use is to call <code>exact (Summable.mul_norm this this : _)</code>, but I wanted to know why I have to do it this way instead of calling <code>exact Summable.mul_norm this this</code>. Here is what I found:</p>\n<p>The statement <code>Summable.mul_norm this this</code> is visually identical to the goal (excluding choice in variable name for the function), but the norm it uses has type <code>instCommCStarAlgebraComplex.toCStarAlgebra.toNorm</code>. I checked the other variables and operators involved, and they all use the same types between the goal and <code>Summable.mul_norm this this</code>. I thought that I couldn't call <code>exact Summable.mul_norm this this</code> directly due to this type difference, but there are two pieces of evidence against this claim:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>I tried using <code>rw[(rfl : NonUnitalSeminormedRing.toSeminormedAddCommGroup.toNorm = instCommCStarAlgebraComplex.toCStarAlgebra.toNorm)]</code> before calling <code>exact Summable.mul_norm this this</code>. This did change the goal, and it now appears that the goal and <code>Summable.mul_norm this this</code> have matching types and formulas. However, this didn't work.</li>\n<li>Entering <code>have h := Summable.mul_norm</code> and then <code>exact h</code> closes the goal without needing to change the type of the norm.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Also, I can call <code>convert Summable.mul_norm this this</code> to resolve the goal, but <a href=\"https://share.google/aimode/32VXqehU0brbjiphl\">Gemini</a> says that this is \"not the best long-term approach\". (Is my current method any better?)</p>\n<p>Why doesn't Lean automatically resolve the typeclass differences? Is this a glitch?</p>",
